  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nAn upper level trough will bring another push of cool and wet  \nweather on Monday and Tuesday. Breezy winds, valley rain, and \nmountain snow will begin late Monday into early Tuesday. Snow\nlevels will start at 5500-6500 feet, dropping to 4500-5500 \nfeet by Tuesday morning. This will bring minor snow accumulation\nto the Central Idaho Mountains and the Idaho-Nevada border, \nwith Tuesday night snowfall totals between 1 to 3 inches. A \nridge of high pressure will briefly build into the region on \nWednesday, bringing mostly dry conditions. \n\nNear to slightly below normal temperatures will continue as cold\nair stays firmly in place through the long term period. Model \nagreement remains high through Friday with a deeper upper level \nlow bringing much colder air and another hit of precipitation to\nthe region late Wednesday into early Friday. Some model \ndisagreement exists with how low snow levels will drop, with \nabout three quarters of the GFS/ECMWF ensemble solutions \nfavoring snow levels dropping to valley floors by Thursday \nnight. This would bring the first measurable snowfall to the \nTreasure Valley for the season. Considerable variability exists \nin the high temperature forecast, with Thursday and Friday highs\nranging from the upper 30s to the upper 40s. Current forecast \nfavors the warmer range of these outcomes, with snow levels only\ndropping to 3000-4000 feet MSL and rain staying the dominant \nprecipitation type for most lower elevation valleys through \nFriday. Precipitation will linger over the region through Friday\nmorning, tapering off as high pressure begins to build back \ninto the region by the weekend.",
